
DESCRIPTION:

firstnum is a terminal command which permits to obtain lists of prime number
and/or prime factor of decomposition from a series indicated as
argument in the same command. Example: firstnum 1000 1030

firstnum have a series of option that work both as filters on the visualisation
and as specific functionality. Example: firstnum -p 1 10000
To get a complete list of available options, run the firstnum command with the
--help option.

first is a terminal command (semplification of firstnum) which permits to know
if is a prime number of a divisble number. It is possible obtain also a list of
prime number into a specified series. Example: first 12345 67899



INSTALLATION:

The installation of the two commands pretty simple, it's enough to follow the steps
below reported.
There are two possible procedures:

Manual procedure:
To manually install first and firstnum copy, from terminal, the relatives files
in a directory of the sistem path like /bin or /usr/bin, or only aswell in the
directory bin or .local/bin of the user directory. Finally copy also the hidden
directory .fnl in the user directory.
Open a shell of terminal, so run in sequence the follow commands:

 $ cd <PATH>/Firstnum-public-109a-20260421
 $ sudo cp -a first /bin/
 $ sudo cp -a  firstnum /bin/
 $ rm -r $HOME/.fnl    (<-- Only run if a previous version is installed.)
 $ cp -R -a .fnl $HOME

Automatic procedure:
To automatic install firstnum and first run in sequence the follow commands:

 $ cd <PATH>/Firstnum-public-109a-20260421
 $ . install.sh

NOTE: <PATH> is the current path = $HOME/<PATH>, which is the path that leads to
the firstnum-public-109a-20260213 directory. You have to go to the current path
otherwise the installation procedure does non work.



UNINSTALLATION:

To completely uninstall firstnum, proceed as follows:

Manual procedure:
To manually uninstall first and firstnum, run the following commands in sequence:

 $ rm $HOME/.fstlang.cfg
 $ rm $HOME/.firstlang.cfg
 $ rm -r $HOME/.fnl
 $ sudo rm /bin/first
 $ sudo rm /bin/firstnum

Automatic procedure:
To automatically uninstall first and firstnum, run the following commands in sequence:

 $ cd <PATH>/Firstnum-public-109a-20260421
 $ . uninstall.sh

NOTE: <PATH> is the current path = $HOME/<PATH>, which is the path that leads to
the firstnum-public-109a-20260213 directory. You have to go to the current path
otherwise the uninstallation procedure does non work.



USE:

To use first and firstnum commands, when installed in the path, type only the name
and refer to the help for operating modality.

Examples: firstnum
          firstnum --help
          firstnum -O
          firstnum 321987
          firstnum -a 65500 65599
          firstnum -aL 1 65535 >> lista.txt
          firstnum -al 65536 131071 >> lista.txt
          firstnum -pn 10000 20000
          firstnum -pc 100 2000



EXTRA:

You can also run firstnum on Windows by pre-enabling WSL (Windows Subsystem Linux)
support. After enabling WSL on Windows via the PowerShell terminal, simply follow
the steps above for a standard GNU/Linux installation.
Please note: firstnum is run in the Windows terminal (PowerShell) after typing the
wsl command and entering the GNU/Linux environment.

In the directory Firstnum-public-109a-20260421 there are also the sources of firstnum
and first in C language.
The same commands are also there with the following names:
firstnum, firstnum109a-20260319, first and first104-20260416 (executable),
firstnum109a-20260319.c, firstnum109a-20260319 (copia).c, first104-20260416.c
and first104-20260416 (copia).c (sources).

To accurately display them adequately prepare gedit(for GNOME) or whatever else
text editor, for example Kate (per KDE) to the view of C source.

Turn on the "visualisation of straight number", "a new paragraph automatic",
"right margin to 80 or 132 column", "don't split words on two lines,
"automatic identification of the type of the text" (highlighting)
which need to be set up on C language; furthermore, much important,
the tabulation need to be corresponding to 3 or 4 spaces,
or alternatively turn on "Insert spaces instead of tabs". If possible turn on also
the highlighting of the right margin and the current line.
(Note: The C sources were written with the gedit editor with the "Insert spaces
instead of tabs" option enabled.)
See the settings of respective editor to turn on the voices if possible.

